00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.150 align:start position:0%
 
my name is lynette mara i'm a graduate  student at ucsf and today i'm  interviewing anita sill  who is a professor at ucsf and is also  an hhmi early career investigator and a  mother of two kids  and a black belt in taekwondo  tell me a little bit more about what  it's like to be an hhmi early career  investigator and how is that different  from  just being an hhmi investigator in  general  so the hhmi investigator program has  been around for a while and is generally  for  you know more established scientists so  a few years ago  hhmi decided that  especially with the  current state of funding that it would  be really that they could have a big  impact if they decided to fund  scientists at an earlier stage of their  career so they had their first  early career scientist competition in  around 2009 and selected 50 people  to be part of this program  it's a six year program  with graduated levels of funding that  increase over that six years  and  unlike the investigator  program it's not a renewable program but  it's designed to give people a real  boost at a time in their career that  could have a lot of impact for them  let's talk about um taekwondo what is  taekwondo all about and how did you get  into it  you know what was the training like yeah  those are great questions so  um form formally i think taekwondo  translates into something that means the  the way of the hand and the foot um and  it's a martial art it's a striking  martial art that involves um  uh you know kicking different types of  kicks  in my there are some different styles of  taekwondo and the in the one that i  learned  we also emphasized a lot of hand  techniques  and we used a sparring style that  included  both the hand techniques to the head as  well as  kicking techniques to the head and other  targets other body targets  and i really just you know i just fell  into taekwondo by accident really so  in the middle of graduate school i was  looking around for a different workout  and  i was a graduate student at ucsf and  here at  milbury union just across the street  from my lab  in the gym they were offering a  taekwondo class for some reason you know  stuck with it even though  i  you know i definitely didn't  definitely didn't get into it with the  idea of getting a black belt and in fact  if you had told me as a beginning  student that i would one day  earn a black belt and teach taekwondo i  would have laughed i mean i would have  not believed that that was  even remotely possible  but you know we had a very supportive  group of people  it was a ucsf studio so we had some  people from the community  but a lot of uh you know ucsf graduate  students and medical students and um so  i guess in some ways it was kind of an  environment of you know nerdy  20 to 40 year olds to begin with and you  know there wasn't it wasn't a  very um  testosterone driven  environment it was a very  it was it was definitely an ambitious  environment in the sense that people  wanted to push  students to  the maximum but in a  you know in in in a really positive kind  of way how do you even get from like  starting out to black belts i'm sure  it's a little bit different in different  dojos or studios in ours we had  we had belt tests that's probably fairly  common  in the taekwondo realm  so  there was kind of a progression of white  belt to yellow belt green belt blue belt  red belt and black belt  and  i think a lot of people made it from  white belt to sort of to green belt kind  of the intermediate stages but then  there were there was  so i think even for those people who  came in with a lot of natural talent at  some point in a martial art you get to a  you get to a stage where  you're going to have to put  a lot of work and effort in to move  beyond that comfort zone and learn to do  things that you  you know there's only so much that comes  naturally before you have to put some  amount of work in it so we tended to  lose people  at that stage for me it took six years  to go in between white belt and black  belt  for other people it was a shorter time  frame and for others it was longer  so you didn't necessarily know  especially as a beginning student when  the next belt test was going to be  because our belt tests were a long  affair so  let's say for an earlier belt you know  was maybe a minimum of you know a  four-hour  test up through black belt which is a  two-day test you can't  they're big time commitments from the  instructor's point of view so they  weren't just it was rarely just one  person um  testing you would get to know beforehand  when your belt test was going to be  which was really important i think  especially for the higher belts because  for me and  the two people from ucsf that i took my  black belt test with we  you know we trained for nine months  about 20 hours a week a lot of that  training together which is a real  which is a real bonding experience for  sure  so what are the tests like like  do you just like kick things or i don't  know what do they involve the nature of  the test is really defined by the  instructor  and i had a few different instructors  over the time that i was a taekwondo  student  but the person that i think had the most  influence on our tests um  was the instructor that i had for the  longest period of time that was  philip moore who's now a professor at  george washington university um  but he he was he is great at uh pushing  people to be the best that that they can  be in his general philosophy of testing  was  that um  if you if you push people to the point  of mental and physical exhaustion then  you can see what they are really capable  of doing  so you know if you're fresh you know  there's a you know there's a number of  kicks or techniques that of course you  can do well but if you really know how  to do them then you should be able to do  them  at the end of you know two days of  physical hardship i think was the idea  so our tests would include running we  would run sets of stairs there's there's  200 steps  between um kirkham and lawton street in  san francisco um on 15th avenue there's  a steep set of stairs outside so we'd  run to those stairs we'd have to run  there  um  you know holding like some of those big  you know exercise balls from the gym  we'd run sets of stairs we'd uh row  um  we'd of course do a lot of kicking  techniques and hand techniques  sparring is a component of belt tests we  do board breaks  you know  formal techniques where there are so the  taekwondo has um  specific forms or orcata where you do a  there's sort of a set of techniques it's  a group of techniques um that are that  you do together  um like a set of  kicks or hand techniques and they get  progressively more complicated as you  move up  and you know learn  different types of techniques  so you spoke a little bit about um how  to manage your schedule can you talk  about how you juggled all those things  yeah i think  i think the juggling is a challenge i i  always like smile internally when people  call it balance because it it doesn't  feel like balanced in any kind of way i  think a lot of it is  compartmentalizing different  um  you know different things at different  times so  you know when i have to make when i know  i'm going to have to go home and make  30 cupcakes because my son is  celebrating his birthday in class the  next day i don't worry about it when i'm  at work i'm like okay that's i'm going  to deal with that later and when one of  my kids is sick  and my husband's with them and i can't  be there and i'm at work  i don't you know i try not to think  about that either i basically have to  i think you know have to be very  organized and  focus on whatever is the task at hand  and get it done  there's a lot i think a lot of it is  figuring out  um how to be really efficient and  what types of things you know what can i  do when i'm tired  and do that then you know what do i have  to be really on the ball to get done and  you know sort of figure out times of the  day  when  you know that are that maximize my  productivity so do you see  a lot of parallels or connections  between your your training and your  scientific career i think they  definitely influenced each other  probably in ways that i didn't fully  appreciate at the time they're very  you know they're very similar journeys  where there's you know a big picture  goal at the end of it but to get there  you have to focus on the day-to-day and  lose some of the anxiety about whether  you know is this ultimately going to add  up to something am i ever going to  graduate you know am i ever going to get  this sidekick to work  you know and you just sort of  have some faith that at least  i'm going to give it the best that i can  and i'm going to take it one step at a  time  and  before you know it you've actually  gotten somewhere that you might not have  imagined you were capable of

